  "conclusions": [
    "Six deterministic real-checkpoint updates match bit-for-bit with cache on/off, independently with expert checkpointing enabled and disabled. Small full-model tests also compare unclipped gradients exactly.",
    "Controlled median throughput gains are modest: about 1.5% checkpointed, 1.9% without expert checkpointing. These are short-run observations, not guaranteed speedups.",
    "Normal nondeterministic checkpointed runs overlap in timing and do not establish a reliable speed improvement. Their learning trajectories also differ; use deterministic pairs for controlled comparison.",
    "Without expert checkpointing peak allocated memory drops by 4.062 GiB (17.828 to 13.766). Checkpointed deterministic peak increases by 0.155 GiB (5.107 to 5.262).",
    "The matched profiled step removes 135 copy_ calls and reduces total copy_ GPU time from 108.63 to 93.41 ms, with identical mm call counts. Instrumented time is not unprofiled throughput or GPU occupancy."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "Finite tests plus local cast-derivative reasoning support equivalence for supported dispatch paths; this is not a universal proof of PyTorch/backend correctness.",
    "No convergence, general capability or long-duration throughput claim. Data is performance input only; no trained checkpoint saved.",
    "Only this RTX 5090/PyTorch/CUDA stack measured. No SM120 custom kernels, BF16 masters, FP8 or NVFP4 introduced.",
    "No expert eviction between a text forward and its backward is introduced or supported. Existing paging/optimizer ownership constraints still apply.",
    "Peak memory excludes initial checkpoint load and includes warmup. New tokens/s uses stride; processed tokens/s uses full context and is twice as large.",
    "Forced CPU BF16 prefix comparisons across different chunk shapes differ by up to 0.001953125 in both cached and uncached paths; outputs match bit-for-bit per shape. Strict FP32 and CUDA BF16 causal regressions pass. The CPU diagnostic does not establish shape-invariant BF16 numerics."
  ]
